Variable Type Content <code>{eventType}</code> string <code>decision.litige-ouvert</code> <code>{source}</code> string the emitter (<code>process:litige-ouvert</code>, <code>connector-engine</code>, …) <code>{entity}</code> string the ingested table, or <code>—</code> <code>{occurredAt}</code> string ISO timestamp <code>{payload}</code> map, navigable the event's payload — <code>{payload.entity_key}</code>, <code>{#for f in payload.fields}</code> <code>{payloadJson}</code> string the same payload, serialized — for <code>&lt;pre&gt;</code> blocks <p>Payloads differ per event type (registry), so guard what may be absent — a missing reference fails the render and the mail is skipped:</p> <pre><code>&lt;h2&gt;{payload.message ?: eventType}&lt;/h2&gt;       {! fallback when the key is absent !}
{#if payload.fields}
  {#for f in payload.fields}&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;{f.key}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;{f.value}&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;{/for}
{/if}
</code></pre> <p><code>decision.*</code> payloads come in three shapes, sharing only <code>message</code> — which is why <code>decision-alert</code> leads with it and guards everything else:</p> <ul> <li>Process rules (<code>decision.&lt;name&gt;</code>): <code>message</code> (the rule's   rendered sentence), <code>process</code>, <code>entity</code>, <code>entity_key</code> — and <code>fields</code> (the row columns   the message referenced, with their values) only when the message references any.</li> <li>Dormancy watches (<code>decision.dormant.&lt;workflow&gt;</code>): <code>message</code>,   <code>workflow</code>, <code>doc_key</code>, <code>state</code>, <code>since</code>, <code>first_observed_at</code>, <code>threshold</code> — never   <code>process</code> or <code>entity_key</code>; <code>fields</code> only when the message cites columns or the workflow   declares some.</li> <li>Due dates (<code>decision.overdue.&lt;workflow&gt;</code>): <code>message</code>, <code>workflow</code>,   <code>doc_key</code>, <code>state</code>, <code>due</code>, <code>due_at</code>; <code>fields</code> on the same terms.</li> </ul>

Key What it says <code>resolution.identity</code> for each source table, the column that identifies one of its rows — one per source, every source listed. Write the column's name in the entity (the right side of the source's <code>map</code>), not the source's own name: <code>t_res_crm: crm_id</code>, although the CRM file calls it <code>contact_id</code> <code>resolution.cascade</code> the rules, tried in order — at least one <code>cascade[].id</code> the rule's name, shown in the history <code>cascade[].exact.fields</code> canonical columns that must be equal — several fields must all be equal <code>cascade[].exact.normalize</code> how values are compared: <code>lower</code>, <code>upper</code>, <code>remove-diacritics</code>, <code>strip-invisible</code>, <code>fix-mojibake</code>, <code>replace-smart-quotes</code>, <code>decode-html-entities</code>, <code>titlecase</code>, <code>clean</code>. Here <code>clean</code> only removes a leading byte-order mark: it does not trim spaces <code>cascade[].propose</code> reserved: no candidate provider ships today. Such a rule leaves its rows unresolved (provider) <p>Rules run in order, and a later rule can settle what an earlier one left ambiguous. Add a second rule on the name: a CRM contact named Levage Lyon at the shared mailbox is ambiguous by email, then resolved to <code>C-004</code> by name. Its history then shows two lines: <code>AMBIGUOUS</code> under <code>email</code>, then <code>RESOLVED</code> under <code>name</code>.</p> <pre><code>cascade:
  - id: email
    exact: { fields: [email], normalize: [lower] }
  - id: name
    exact: { fields: [name], normalize: [lower] }
</code></pre> <ul> <li><code>match:</code> and <code>resolution:</code> are two ways to fuse; an entity declares one or the other.</li> <li><code>_entity_id</code>, <code>_resolution</code>, <code>_source_id</code>, <code>_handle</code> and <code>_canonical_key</code> are reserved column names.</li> <li>A resolved entity is read like any other: <code>lm entity get</code>, <code>lm view</code>,   <code>lm ask</code>, an export, a SearchIndex.</li> </ul>

Field Required Meaning <code>kind</code> yes must be <code>Entity</code> <code>name</code> yes a simple identifier (letters, digits, <code>_</code>), at most 61 characters — becomes the view <code>connector.v_&lt;name&gt;</code>, lower-cased, and the name the chat advertises <code>label</code> no the entity's human name — a <code>kind: View</code> that declares no <code>title:</code> is headed with it, and the schema card shows it beside the view name. Without one, both fall back to <code>name</code> <code>description</code> no free text for humans <code>match</code> yes (≥ 1) the canonical column(s) that identify the same real-world row across sources; several make a composite key. Every source must map every match column, in the same case <code>sources[]</code> yes (≥ 1) ordered — the order is the merge priority; the first source wins a disagreement, unless the column names its own (<code>columns.&lt;col&gt;.prefer</code>) <code>sources[].table</code> yes the source's dynamic table in the <code>connector</code> schema, without the schema prefix (<code>t_acme_desk_erp</code>) <code>sources[].map</code> yes (≥ 1) <code>source column: canonical column</code>, per source <code>joins[]</code> no relational lookups that enrich the fused rows — see Fuse or join. Never fused, never add a row <code>joins[].table</code> yes the lookup's dynamic table in the <code>connector</code> schema; it must not also be a source <code>joins[].from</code> yes a canonical column of the fused entity (it may be a <code>match</code> column) — the value looked up <code>joins[].to</code> yes a physical column of the lookup table — the key it is matched on (it need not appear in <code>map</code>) <code>joins[].map</code> yes (≥ 1) <code>lookup column: canonical column</code>; the new canonical names must not collide with any source column, any other join, or a reserved name, and none may be a <code>match</code> column <code>scopes</code> no the business scopes whose chat and views may read this entity — an entity with none is not discoverable by anyone <code>columns</code> no per-canonical-column grounding <ul> <li>Column order: every <code>sources[].map</code> value in source order, then every <code>joins[].map</code> value   in declared order, then <code>_conflicts</code>.</li> <li>Reserved names: <code>_conflicts</code>, <code>_src</code>, <code>_ord</code>, <code>_entity_id</code>, <code>_resolution</code>, <code>_source_id</code>,   <code>_handle</code>, <code>_canonical_key</code>.</li> <li>Case: <code>email</code> in one source and <code>Email</code> in another is refused, not split into two columns.</li> <li>Every value reads as a string: <code>credit_limit</code> reads <code>"500"</code>, not <code>500</code>. A column every source   holds as a date or a timestamp stays one — see Dates across sources — and   one every source holds as a number stays a number, so it orders and compares as one — see   Numbers across sources. Any other column is text: cast where you consume.</li> </ul> <p>The key set is closed: an unknown key such as <code>scope:</code> for <code>scopes:</code> is refused, not dropped:</p> <pre><code>$ lm entity apply -f docs/apps/demo/entities/63-refused-unknown-key.yaml
Error: API error 400: unknown key(s) in the manifest: scope — kind:Entity honors root {apiVersion, columns, description, joins, kind, label, match, name, resolution, scopes, sources}, sources[] {map, table}, joins[] {from, map, table, to}, resolution {cascade, identity}, resolution.cascade[] {exact, id, propose}, resolution.cascade[].exact {fields, normalize}, resolution.cascade[].propose {acceptAbove, provider, reviewAbove}
</code></pre>

Field Required Meaning <code>metadata.name</code> yes the view's name — in the store, lower-cased, a URL-safe slug (<code>[a-z0-9_-]</code>, starting with a letter or digit) <code>spec.scope</code> yes the scope whose schema card backs the view <code>spec.table</code> no defaults to the scope's single table; required when the scope has several. Raw <code>connector.t_*</code> tables and fused <code>connector.v_*</code> views both qualify <code>spec.title</code> no the display title — falls back to the entity's <code>label:</code>, then the table's name <code>spec.columns[]</code> yes (≥ 1) the columns to show, in order. Every one must exist in the scope's card. The first is the sort order and the detail key <code>spec.links[]</code> no cells that lead somewhere: another view, or an external page — see Linking views <p><code>apply</code> refuses a key it does not know:</p> <pre><code>$ lm view apply -f docs/apps/demo/views/61-refused-unknown-key.yaml
Error: API error 400: unknown key(s) in the manifest: spec.titel — kind:View honors root {apiVersion, kind, metadata, spec}, metadata {name}, spec {columns, links, scope, table, title}, spec.links[] {column, filters, targetView, url}
</code></pre> <p><code>lm view -f</code> still renders such a file: the unknown key is ignored, so this one shows under the table's name instead of a title.</p>

Field Required Meaning <code>metadata.name</code> yes kebab-case identifier (<code>[a-z0-9-]+</code>) — the name <code>apply</code> updates under, and the alert's <code>decision.dormant.&lt;name&gt;</code> <code>spec.scope</code> yes the business scope the lifecycle belongs to; must be a discovered scope — below <code>spec.provenance</code> yes must be <code>declared</code> — the only accepted value; the chat cites it <code>spec.on.table</code> yes the table to watch — an ingest table (<code>connector.t_&lt;table&gt;</code>) or a fused entity view (<code>connector.v_&lt;entity&gt;</code>, always lower-case) <code>spec.on.column</code> yes the column whose values this taxonomy explains — whatever the source called it <code>spec.on.key</code> no (required by <code>dormancy</code> and <code>due</code>) the business key linking a document's successive versions <code>spec.on.order</code> no (required by <code>dormancy</code> on a fused view) the column ordering a document's versions: a date, a timestamp, a number, or ISO 8601 text with <code>Z</code> or an offset — requires <code>spec.on.key</code>, below <code>spec.check.trigger</code> no <code>ingestion</code>: re-check the declaration after each completed run that writes <code>spec.on.table</code> — below. The apply always checks; any other value is refused <code>spec.dormancy.after</code> no (required by <code>dormancy</code>) the sweep threshold, <code>[0-9]+[mhd]</code> (<code>30d</code>) — requires <code>spec.on.key</code>, and <code>spec.on.order</code> on a fused view <code>spec.dormancy.message</code> no the alert text, in the team's language: <code>{doc_key}</code>, <code>{state}</code>, <code>{state_label}</code>, <code>{since}</code>, <code>{first_observed_at}</code>, <code>{threshold}</code>, <code>{workflow}</code>; any other <code>{name}</code> is a column of <code>spec.on.table</code>, checked at apply when the table exists — what a decision carries. Absent: the English default below <code>spec.dormancy.fields</code> no columns of <code>spec.on.table</code> sent in the payload's <code>fields</code> and not in the sentence (<code>[owner_email]</code>) <code>spec.due.column</code> no (required by <code>due</code>) the column whose date is the document's deadline — an ingest table or a fused view <code>spec.due.states</code> no (required by <code>due</code>) the declared, non-terminal state codes in which a passed date makes the document overdue (<code>[CONFIRMED]</code>); an alias, an undeclared or a terminal state is refused <code>spec.due.zone</code> no the time zone a date-only column is read in (<code>Europe/Paris</code>): due at the end of that day there. Absent: <code>UTC</code>. An unknown zone is refused <code>spec.due.message</code> no the alert's sentence: <code>{doc_key}</code>, <code>{state}</code>, <code>{state_label}</code>, <code>{due}</code>, <code>{workflow}</code>; any other <code>{name}</code> is a column of <code>spec.on.table</code>, checked at apply when the table exists <code>spec.due.fields</code> no columns of <code>spec.on.table</code> sent in the payload's <code>fields</code> and not in the sentence <code>spec.states[]</code> yes (≥ 1) the declared states <code>spec.transitions[]</code> no the allowed edges of the graph <p><code>states[]</code> — <code>code</code> (required; unique, case- and trim-insensitive), <code>label</code>, <code>aliases[]</code> (the dirty spellings that resolve to this code), <code>initial</code> (exactly one state must set it <code>true</code>), <code>terminal</code> (a terminal state may carry no outgoing transition).</p> <p><code>transitions[]</code> — <code>from[]</code> (one edge can fan in from several states) and <code>to</code>.</p> <p>An unknown key is refused, at every level:</p> <pre><code>$ lm workflow apply -f docs/apps/demo/workflows/67-refused-unknown-key.yaml
Error: API error 400: unknown key(s) in the manifest: spec.after — kind:Workflow honors root {apiVersion, kind, metadata, spec}, metadata {name}, spec {check, dormancy, due, on, provenance, scope, states, transitions}, spec.on {column, key, order, table}, spec.check {trigger}, spec.dormancy {after, fields, message}, spec.due {column, fields, message, states, zone}, states[] {aliases, code, initial, label, terminal}, transitions[] {from, to}
</code></pre>
